# Local biomedical image review workspace

**Research proposal · 6 October 2026**

## Recommendation

Build a local-first, single-writer desktop project workspace with a thin shell around the napari viewer/layer model, a project and annotation model owned by the group, and a small set of explicitly supported readers. Investigate these exact candidate pins: OME-NGFF 0.5.0, Zarr-Python v3.4.0 (`292aeb4edcbbff50ec0ef7a49ff376e267904efc`), `tifffile` v2026.9.20 (`a14e516c4fa3c0c9ede980ebb3111260347dd213`) and napari v0.9.2 (`595639295a153c84bcb127a9e07d801355440a35`). If Dask is included for chunked writes, its v2025.11.0 release (`7ffe1af8192d603fdd1f564626243f5c37075190`) includes the fix discussed below. These are research pins for an integration spike, not a tested compatible lockfile. Keep MVP writes serial and unsharded until application-level checks pass.

Use OME-Zarr 0.5 for supported label-array and derived-array exchange, while preserving richer project coordinate metadata when the standard is insufficient. Keep imported originals read-only and external by default. Treat viewer, readers and storage format as replaceable components behind a versioned project contract. This plan meets the requested workflows in stages; it does not assert that any component already meets the 3 GB, 16 GB or responsiveness targets.

Evidence labels below distinguish **source facts**, **engineering inferences**, **product choices**, and **proposed validation**. The source catalog provides exact pins, locators and capture identities. Citations identify mechanisms and their limits; they do not establish whole-application behavior.

## Findings that shape the design

1. **OME-NGFF supplies a useful multidimensional storage contract, with a material transform limit.** OME-NGFF 0.5.0 specifies Zarr v3 images and arrays with declared axes and per-level coordinate transformations. Multiscale image dimensions are ordered as time, channel/custom axes, then spatial axes; space may be 2D or 3D. Its defined transformations are scale followed by optional translation; this model does not encode arbitrary rotation or shear. Label images use integer sample types. [S1, “Images”, “axes”, “coordinateTransformations”, “multiscales”, and “labels”](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/ome/ngff-spec/0.5/index.md)

   **Inference:** keep complete coordinate meaning in the project contract. An export that cannot represent a stored transform must include an explicit sidecar or perform a separately recorded resampling operation.

2. **`tifffile` documents a practical lazy-access path for TIFF, not universal TIFF support or a performance guarantee.** Its pinned README demonstrates reading OME-TIFF metadata/series, exposing image data as a Zarr array and slicing a region; it also describes TIFF subsets, vendor extensions and codec dependencies. [S2, `README.rst`, usage and support notes](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/cgohlke/tifffile/v2026.9.20/README.rst)

   **Inference:** use it for a narrowly tested OME-TIFF subset, keep source files untouched and refuse layouts whose smallest readable segment exceeds the memory budget. Lazy array access alone does not prove bounded decoding for every TIFF page, codec or segment.

3. **napari Shapes is a separate GUI/annotation precedent.** The v0.9.2 `Shapes` layer accepts vertex arrays in 2D or 3D, multiple geometry types, style/attribute state and affine layer transforms. This contributes a reusable drawing/viewer mechanism independent of NGFF's on-disk axis/calibration schema. [S3, `src/napari/layers/shapes/shapes.py`, Shapes class documentation and constructor](https://github.com/napari/napari/blob/v0.9.2/src/napari/layers/shapes/shapes.py)

   **Limit:** this source does not prove our project shell, save/reopen semantics, accessibility, undo policy, responsiveness, or every reader integration. Pin v0.9.2 for an integration spike, not as an adopted dependency before it passes.

4. **A real chunked-write failure shows why storage granularity and task scheduling must agree.** Zarr-Python issue #3514 reported a Dask `to_zarr` write over sharded data losing values: a reported 3D sum was 45,004,136 before writing and 25,746,614 on readback. The discussion identified concurrent writes to different chunks inside a shared shard as unsafe without coordination. Dask PR #12105 changed `to_zarr` to use a Zarr array's shard shape, when available, as its Dask write/rechunk unit (falling back to chunks), and added `test_zarr_chunk_shards_mismatch_warns`: a small 1D mismatch test that checks rechunk shape and readback equality. The merge commit is `d5b07164359d3377fd9d2d0e7eebd134f9a3c31a`. The Dask v2025.11.0 release commit is `7ffe1af8192d603fdd1f564626243f5c37075190`, and GitHub's ancestry comparison shows the merge commit is included. [S4 issue #3514](https://github.com/zarr-developers/zarr-python/issues/3514) · [S5 discussion and later report](https://api.github.com/repos/zarr-developers/zarr-python/issues/3514/comments) · [S6 PR #12105](https://github.com/dask/dask/pull/12105) · [S7 changed implementation and test](https://api.github.com/repos/dask/dask/pulls/12105/files) · [S8–S10 release ancestry](https://api.github.com/repos/dask/dask/compare/d5b07164359d3377fd9d2d0e7eebd134f9a3c31a...7ffe1af8192d603fdd1f564626243f5c37075190)

   **Limit and consequence:** the focused test establishes the intended aligned-shard case at the merged Dask commit; it is not proof of safe concurrent writes for all shapes/stores. A January 2026 issue comment reports a related 3D corruption result still present for an awkward shape (expected sum 45,004,136; readback 21,409,496). The available chain does not diagnose that later case. For the first version, write unsharded arrays and serialize project writes. If sharding is later needed, test aligned and irregular edge chunks with exact readback on the actual pinned stack. This is evidence about one Dask/Zarr write path, not proof that OME-NGFF or all Zarr writes are defective.

The precedents are independently useful: NGFF defines interoperable array metadata; `tifffile` provides an image-file reader path; napari supplies viewer/geometry interaction; Dask's issue/fix/test chain informs write scheduling. Their combination here is an architecture proposal, not a demonstrated integrated system.

## Supported input boundary for the first useful version

**Product choice:** support local OME-TIFF and OME-Zarr 0.5 only, read-only, with tested package/codec combinations. Accept one selected image series/group with a 2D or 3D spatial image and optional time and channel axes, using tested integer or floating sample types. For OME-Zarr, require axis order compliant with its v0.5 specification (time, then channel/custom axes, then spatial axes; e.g. `TCZYX`, with absent dimensions omitted), valid metadata and bounded, unsharded chunks. For OME-TIFF, accept only an OME-XML axis/series mapping that is unambiguous; its stored order may differ, so preserve the exact source order and explicit mapping rather than forcing NGFF order. For TIFF, require a tested tile/strip organization whose largest decoded read unit is at most 64 MiB and a present decoder for its compression. Establish whether these caps and reader APIs work in the implementation spike.

These are product boundary choices, not capabilities promised by the standards or reader documentation. Exclude proprietary microscope formats, unknown/custom axes, ambiguous series, unknown channel/sample axes, RGB/sample axes in this first scope, unsupported codecs, invalid/contradictory calibration, NGFF transformations the importer cannot interpret, and read units exceeding the cap. Broader formats enter only with real fixtures and a named maintainer.

**Import review and failure behavior:** inspect headers and metadata first, then show source series, shape/type, original axes, pixel size/unit, orientation/transform, codec/layout and reader version. When an axis or calibration is ambiguous, preserve the original metadata and offer explicit axis mapping and calibration correction with reason and timestamp. Never silently squeeze/reorder/flip axes or guess units. If neither unambiguous interpretation nor user correction is available, mark that image import rejected/unsupported and identify the blocking field; the rest of the project stays usable. Import into a staging record and promote only a fully validated record into the active generation. The original image bytes are never rewritten.

## Project data contract

A versioned project directory contains a manifest, immutable source records, annotation records, derived-operation records, optional label-array stores, style definitions and recoverable save generations. It is independent of viewer in-memory state.

- **Identity and provenance:** each logical image has a stable project UUID plus source format, series/group selection, original path/URI, byte length, file timestamps as hints, exact source axes/shape/dtype, untouched raw source metadata, reader/package versions, import time and integrity state. Compute SHA-256 by streaming fixed-size blocks in a cancellable background task. For a directory source such as OME-Zarr, record sorted relative paths, sizes, per-file hashes and a digest of that inventory. Filename, path, shape and mtime are hints, not identity. Until the content digest is complete, say “identity pending”, not “verified”. One file may be two logical acquisitions with different project IDs and a shared content hash.
- **Relocation and portability:** reference external originals by default so a 3 GB source is not silently copied. On another machine, leave missing sources offline until the researcher selects a replacement; verify its streaming content hash before reconnecting. Same path with changed bytes is a mismatch. Offer an explicit portable bundle that copies unchanged sources and records digests, trading storage and transfer time for self-containment. Copy closed bundles to shared storage; work on a local copy. A bundle with external references can reopen only if those sources are available or explicitly relocated.
- **Axes and coordinates:** preserve `array_axes` exactly as read, in source order, e.g. `TCZYX`; never treat display order as stored order. Spatial coordinates in annotations use canonical source-pixel axes `x,y` or `x,y,z`, with integer index 0 at the center of the first pixel/voxel. A per-image coordinate frame declares dimensionality, axis mapping, physical units and a float64 homogeneous matrix `index_to_world` using column vectors: `[x,y[,z],1] -> physical coordinates`. The matrix includes sampling, origin and orientation and may represent rotation/shear. Record the matrix convention and validate finite, invertible values. Keep non-spatial context (`T`, `C`, and for 2D-on-volume annotations `Z`) as named integer selectors. The status area shows index and physical coordinates/units and active `T/C/Z`. Witness W1 checks the arithmetic on one proposed mapping; it does not validate a reader or viewer.
- **Annotations:** each record has UUID, geometry kind (point, path, polygon, integer mask or acquisition-level note), coordinate-frame ID, vertices or mask reference, class/label, reusable style ID, scope, named selectors, optional author/note and edit history. A slice annotation stores 2D `x,y` vertices and explicit `Z` plus any `T/C`; a volume annotation stores 3D vertices and any selected `T/C`; an acquisition-wide record has no implicit slice/time and may be non-spatial. Never infer scope from the displayed plane. Store masks as integer label data with an explicit label table and frame/scope. Styles are named project/user templates, not baked into source samples.
- **Calibration corrections:** keep raw imported metadata unchanged beside a separate override with old/new values, axes, units, author, timestamp and explanation. The override changes project interpretation and exports, not source bytes. Show a “corrected” marker and provide both values for inspection.
- **Display is a view:** channels on/off, colors, per-channel window/level, LUT/gamma, blend, slice and time are project view settings. Apply intensity mapping to display buffers only, e.g. `clip((sample-low)/(high-low), 0, 1)`; never overwrite or cast source arrays. Visually distinguish original image, annotation layers, display composites and derived-image layers. W1 checks the stated mapping on three values and confirms its input list is unchanged; it is mathematical only.
- **Geometric operations:** crop, reorientation, registration, projection, resampling or other sample-changing operations create a child dataset and never edit the original. Record operation ID, parent/source digest, parameters, input/output axes/shapes/dtypes, output digest, interpolation and border policy, coordinate mapping and exact software pins. Write NGFF scale/translation only when it faithfully represents the transform. Preserve richer transforms in project metadata/sidecar or require explicit resampling to a supported grid.

## Application structure and minimum workflow

1. **Create/open.** Create a versioned project and starting generation; explain whether images will be referenced or copied. On schema migration, write a new generation and preserve the old.
2. **Import/review.** Probe metadata without decoding the full image. Show raw metadata, parsed axes/calibration, codec, sampling and provenance. An unsupported image may remain as a rejected import record with source path and error; existing images and annotations remain intact.
3. **Inspect.** Use bounded Zarr-backed tile/plane reads, cancellable workers and an LRU cache capped at 512 MiB. Limit each decoded unit to 64 MiB in the supported subset. Cache entries are disposable and keyed by source digest, axes/indices and view request. A 3 GB source must not require a full-source copy or full-volume decode just to display a plane. This is a target to measure, not a consequence of using lazy arrays.
4. **Navigate/annotate.** Show `T`, `C`, `Z` controls and keyboard shortcut hints. Use napari Shapes for initial point/path/polygon interaction if the v0.9.2 integration spike verifies dimensions and affine behavior; the project schema, not widget state, is saved truth. Masks are integer label arrays. Record edits as commands with before/after values or changed chunks so undo/redo avoids full-mask snapshots. Include reusable style presets and remappable keys. Make the workflow keyboard operable with clear focus order, persistent metadata/errors, readable text and visible focus.
5. **Save/close.** Append committed project/annotation commands to an edit journal. Save by writing a new version to a staging directory, checksumming contents, flushing files/directories where supported, renaming a complete immutable version into place, then atomically replacing a small `CURRENT` pointer on a tested local filesystem. Preserve the prior version. On startup validate `CURRENT`, checksums and journal prefix; offer the last known-good version and valid journal replay if a save was interrupted. Ignore/quarantine incomplete staging versions. Caches are not authoritative data.
6. **Reopen/transfer.** Reopen the last committed version; verify external sources, report missing/changed hashes and allow explicit relocation. Detect stale project revision and refuse silent overwrite. Shared storage is for closed bundle transfer, not concurrent writes. Do not promise atomic rename, locking or fsync semantics on every network share; qualify them on target storage or stage/copy and verify closed bundles locally.
7. **Export/verify.** Export long-form CSV rows with project/image IDs, content digest, annotation IDs, vertex order, index and physical coordinates/units, frame ID/matrix, scope/selectors, labels/styles and schema version. Masks export as integer label data plus label table and complete frame/provenance sidecar; CSV does not contain mask voxels. A 2D PNG overlay is flattened view output, not a substitute for mask data. Export a small OME-TIFF crop of source samples, preserving dtype and supported selected axes; its sidecar states crop bounds, updated origin, calibration, source digest and whether samples changed. If a user chooses windowed PNG or resampled output, state that it changes samples and record the operation. Build exports in a temporary sibling directory and publish only after data, hashes and sidecar agree. Show a before/after summary: original hash unchanged; source layer untouched; what output samples/axes/calibration were retained, dropped or transformed.

For the six researchers, exchange closed versioned bundles and use one active writer per project copy. Simultaneous edits in a shared folder, conflict-free merge and audit-grade multi-user locking are outside scope. Keep the source digest and project revision with every export so a reviewer can detect annotations associated with different image bytes.

## Atomicity and failure model

Imports, saves and exports are staged away from the active pointer/final path. A crash before promotion leaves the prior project/export intact; startup integrity checks offer recovery and retain incomplete stages for diagnosis. Journal entries carry sequence numbers/checksums; replay only a valid prefix. Keep at least two completed generations until a later verified save. Do not mutate originals or rely on replacing a non-empty directory atomically. Rename/flush guarantees vary, especially on SMB/NFS; if the shared filesystem fails fault-injection checks, use it only for closed, checksummed bundle transfer and work locally.

Source identity is content, not filename. Stream hashes without holding source bytes in memory. Hashing can take time, so show progress/cancel and retain pending/unverified state; require verification before reconnecting moved data or asserting an export matches it. For directory stores, verify deterministic relative paths and bytes. If a source changes, preserve annotations/project state but disable overlay/export association until the user imports a replacement as a new source or explicitly revises provenance.

## Exports: what survives

- **CSV:** point/vector geometry, index and physical coordinates, scope/selectors, IDs, source digest and frame transform. It carries no source pixels or mask voxels.
- **Integer label store:** mask samples and label IDs on declared grid; include axes, scale/translation when representable, frame matrix and source digest. NGFF 0.5 integer labels fit this role [S1], but an arbitrary affine may need a project sidecar.
- **Flattened overlay:** convenience rendering for a selected plane/channel/time and view mapping. It loses independent layers and may omit calibration without its sidecar; mark “flattened preview”.
- **Small derived crop:** default copies selected source samples unchanged, preserving dtype; spatial extent and origin change, not other values. The OME-TIFF/sidecar state subset and transforms. If an export codec cannot encode the supported dtype/calibration without conversion, fail clearly or offer an explicitly labeled converted preview.

## Opportunity and alternative

**Opportunity:** stable image identity plus explicit axes/transforms and OME label output makes project data inspectable and exchangeable. A plugin-oriented viewer lets the group add tested importers incrementally without promising every vendor format; a local manifest/sidecar retains detail hidden by flattened pictures.

**Plausible alternative:** a purpose-built PySide6 shell with pyqtgraph/vispy gives tighter control over keyboard flow, project lifecycle and supported dimensions, but leaves the team responsible for rendering, transforms, drawing and performance fixes. Choose it only if napari integration fails keyboard accessibility, transform mapping, memory or latency gates. OME-TIFF is another useful export for small crops and wider software interchange, but it is less suited to the authoritative chunked-mask/project-history store; keep it as an export target.

## Useful optional leads

Keep institutional storage and multi-user organization (for example OMERO), registration, automatic segmentation, GPU acceleration, concurrent collaboration/version merge and sharded Zarr writes outside minimum scope. Consider them only after the local workflow is reliable and users identify a need. Registration/segmentation must create provenance-recorded derived operations; GPU use must respond to measured bottlenecks; sharding must pass multiwriter/edge-shape checks. See `leads.json` for adoption gates and unresolved dependencies.

## Validation plan — application-level checks proposed / UNEXECUTED

1. **Import/axes corpus:** make small OME-TIFF and NGFF fixtures for every accepted axis shape, dtype, unit, codec and layout. Check metadata-only probe, exact sample readback, coordinate mapping, raw-metadata preservation, explicit correction and clear rejection for unknown axes/series, unsupported codec and oversized read unit. Add real lab files before widening support.
2. **3 GB bounded access:** on an 8-core/16 GB workstation browse a 3 GB supported source while measuring RSS, decoded read sizes and cache use. Proposed gates: cache ≤512 MiB; no decode request >64 MiB; no source-sized second allocation; process peak <6 GiB; cached plane/slice navigation p95 <200 ms; cold selected-plane first draw <2 s; cancel visible/responsive within 1 s at checkpoints. These are proposed acceptance targets, not facts. If a read layout violates them, exclude it or change reader.
3. **Coordinate/export discrimination:** use a small 2D and 3D array with nonzero origin, anisotropic sampling, axis permutation and rotation/shear. Check known index-to-world values and inverse round trips to ≤1e-9 physical-unit error. Export CSV plus label/overlay; parse independently and verify source digest binding. Confirm unrepresentable affine survives in sidecar or export stops pending explicit resampling. W1 checks only the stated arithmetic case.
4. **Display versus geometry:** change channel windows and confirm original byte hash and dtype remain constant; then crop and nearest-neighbor resample a derived child and verify samples, origin and operation lineage. Check time-, slice-, volume- and acquisition-scoped annotations after close/reopen.
5. **Persistence/recovery:** inject failures before/after version promotion, `CURRENT` replacement, journal append, import validation and export publication on local storage and each chosen shared filesystem. Verify last-good recovery, incomplete-stage handling, hashes, committed edits and unchanged source bytes. Simulate relocation, same-path changed bytes and missing/offline sources.
6. **Writer risk:** reproduce the Dask #12105 aligned-shard test with the chosen lock and add a 3D irregular-edge/multiple-writer test. Compare every readback sample; repeat under parallel scheduling and cancellation. Until checks pass, disable sharding and parallel project writes.
7. **Interaction/accessibility:** complete create/import/review/navigate/annotate/undo-redo/save/close/reopen/export using keyboard only. Check focus order, shortcut discoverability, contrast, metadata legibility and persistent textual errors.

W1 is the only candidate-executed check in this research. Its code/input, expected behavior, stdout/stderr/exit and limits are in `witnesses.json`. It checks pure arithmetic/display mapping in isolated Python, not TIFF, Zarr, napari or application behavior. Every other check above is proposed/UNEXECUTED.
